00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:11.510
So microservices
are pretty awesome.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.440
The main objective
about microservices

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.830
is to fundamentally
increase developer velocity

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:17.588
and productivity.

00:02:17.588 --> 00:02:18.630
So this is the key thing.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.480
What we hear all the
time consistently is,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.870
I want a common
development pipeline that

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.467
works for all my environments.

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:29.550
But I also want to be able
to get new capabilities

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.720
into the hands of my
customers as soon as possible

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.092
and do that very, very safely,
and really reduce risk.

00:02:37.092 --> 00:02:38.550
So there are a
bunch of things here

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:43.445
that microservices allow you
to do that makes things better.

00:02:43.445 --> 00:02:44.820
The challenge with
microservices,

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.860
though, is they
increase complexity.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.050
So with that
increased complexity,

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:53.250
you start getting
some additional things

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:57.650
you need to do in order to
manage those microservices.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:02.580
So things like orchestration
becomes important.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.740
Understanding how your
services get connected

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.490
becomes important.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.710
So you actually need a
high level of abstraction.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:14.150
This is where, again, Kubernetes
gets you partway there,

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.090
but Istio or service
mesh can actually help.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.740
That provides at a high
level of abstraction.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.580
So let's look at a--

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.997
so I actually have a really
pretty basic application here.

00:03:22.997 --> 00:03:25.540
This is a demo app.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.250
And if I had a software
developer first

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.343
join a development
team, and we're

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:31.760
looking at building
an application

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.718
or we had an application
already in production,

00:03:33.718 --> 00:03:35.718
I'd probably-- people
would get on a whiteboard,

00:03:35.718 --> 00:03:37.310
and I'd start drawing stuff out.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.130
And you'd end up with
a slimmed-down version

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.850
of something like this
that talks about what

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.710
that application looks like.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:48.260
And even in this very,
very simple example,

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.850
we have around 10
microservices, which

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:51.290
you may be running
many instances

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:55.183
of from a back-end perspective.

00:03:55.183 --> 00:03:56.600
And here, we
actually have a bunch

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:03.040
written in Go, some written
in Python, one in Java.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.870
This actually gives
you the ability

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:08.800
now to deploy certain services
independently of [? other. ?]

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.570
I can scale, for
example, my front

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.050
end independently to
scaling my CheckoutService.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.899
I can patch or release
AdService independently

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:17.954
of other services.

00:04:17.954 --> 00:04:19.329
But I've also done
another thing.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:22.150
I've actually created
complexity around managing

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.752
all these services, and also
managing the communication

00:04:24.752 --> 00:04:25.960
paths between these services.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.940
So we'll look at--

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.652
if I look at the
services themselves

00:04:33.652 --> 00:04:35.110
and actually managing
those, I have

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.510
to now secure the
communications between them.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.300
I have to protect the services,
put rate limits in place.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.600
I don't necessarily want a
service hitting another service

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.750
beyond its load capacity, so I
can put rate limits in place.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.650
I also need to
monitor the health

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.980
of the actual microservices and
understand the dependencies.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.480
One of the things
that you'll hear--

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.930
putting SLOs, or
service-level objectives,

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.720
on a specific service
is very helpful.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.090
And it gives you
some guideline--

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.040
and we'll talk about this--

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:12.140
around what's a best
practice on doing that.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:15.310
But also, understanding
your service dependencies

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.800
and understanding
what the critical user

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.430
journey is across your
environment becomes important.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.380
You'll actually
hear people start

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.690
talking about an SLO on the
critical customer journey

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.740
through my environment.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:33.280
And I'll give you a
very specific example.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.290
If you're just looking
at this for a second,

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.510
if front end goes down, I
actually have an outage.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:39.130
That's a big deal.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.710
If AdService goes down,
or RecommendationService

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.230
goes down, those things--

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:47.020
do I wake up a person
on call for that?

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.160
Maybe not.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.740
So actually, really
understanding

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.320
the dependency map
within your microservices

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:57.730
becomes very important to
understand which ones really

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:00.700
impact your
customer-critical journeys.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.170
So service mesh
is really about--

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.070
service mesh or
Istio really empowers

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.190
developers and operators
to focus on their business

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.050
and modernize and secure
their communications.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:23.190
So this effectively
puts a lot of the toil

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:24.810
in looking after
the interconnect

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.090
between microservices
and securing them

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.430
into the infrastructure.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:32.640
So your service
developers don't have

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.810
to worry about instrumenting
specific things

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:38.190
within their code.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:42.810
They don't have to worry
about creating retries.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.320
They don't have to worry
about doing circuit-breaking.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.820
This actually becomes part of
the [INAUDIBLE] infrastructure

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:48.510
itself.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.250
You want to secure
those services,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:54.180
you can start enabling things
like mTLS between your services

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.260
based on strong
service identity.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.910
So this actually allows
you to take control and get

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.620
a really good
understanding of how

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.256
your services are connected.

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:12.060
One thing Google is
good at is, they operate

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.310
distributed systems at scale.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:17.812
There has been a lot
of effort around--

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:19.520
there's been a book
published around SRE.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.800
But also, just look at--

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.710
the key goal of an
SRE organization

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:27.950
is to basically scale
and scale the services

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.930
that you're running
within your environment,

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.660
but also make them
resilient, reliable,

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:37.290
and provide a very good
end-user experience.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.953
So the question, how does
that map to your organization?

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:49.400
Let's talk a little bit about
the three key pillars for SRE.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:53.380
The three key pillars
really map to SLIs, which

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:57.250
are Service-Level Indicators.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.950
We have SLOs, service-level
objectives, and SLAs.

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:02.200
I'm going to talk a little
bit briefly about all three

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.480
of these and how
they're different

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.650
and also why they're important.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.400
So that one of the key reasons--

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:10.580
so if you think of--

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.840
one of the things
that Istio provides,

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.550
or service mesh
provides, is they

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:16.650
provide consistent
flow logs and metrics

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.360
across your entire environment.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.397
These give you what we would
consider to be golden signals.

00:08:21.397 --> 00:08:23.730
These are things that you can
look at from an error rate

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.190
perspective for latency
across your environment.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:30.060
These become the key metrics
that you use in order

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:33.679
to construct your SLOs on.

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:36.110
SLOs tend to be
targets that you would

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:42.809
map to business service-level
objectives for your team.

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:45.380
So I've given-- the development
team would basically

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.750
look at what the
objectives they want around

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:51.290
uptime, around latencies,
for a specific service.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:54.180
And you tend to do that over
a rolling period of time.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.230
And we'll talk also a little
bit about error budgets

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:59.373
as we move forward.

00:08:59.373 --> 00:09:01.040
SLAs, though, is a
little bit different.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.160
SLA tends to be more
a business agreement

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.360
that you map to your customer.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:10.290
So let's compare and contrast
SLOs versus SLAs for a minute.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.320
So an SLO, to me, it
might be something

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:16.260
that I want to run on a
rolling period of time

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.140
over a week or a month.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.150
But for an SLA, I
actually want to map it

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.890
to a contractual obligation.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:26.210
So for that-- might
be important here is--

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:29.390
if I have an agreement for
my customers that I'm having

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:33.320
a certain amount of
availability for a given month,

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.320
I want to make sure that I'm--

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.500
if my error budget is
getting low for that month,

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.360
maybe I don't roll
out a version of code.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:44.870
Maybe I actually
hold back and wait.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:50.150
So I'm actually using the
error budget for an SLO

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.550
to give me business
indicators as to whether

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.730
or not I want to take the
risk to roll out something

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.195
from a business perspective.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.540
And I think this is where-- so
we've sort of talked a little

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:07.430
bit about SLOs and SLAs--

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:09.980
but I think this is actually
where service mesh can really

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.600
come in and help.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.770
There is a lot of things that
you effectively get for free,

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:17.720
from an infrastructure
perspective, when

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.760
looking at a service mesh.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.960
So let's touch base
on the service mesh.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.480
So the way that we tend to
talk about a service mesh

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:34.660
is, it's effectively a way to
connect and secure services

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:37.960
from a network traffic
perspective automatically.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:43.390
It gives you-- another key thing
with Istio or service mesh is,

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:45.490
it does it in a
language-independent way.

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:48.130
It doesn't matter what version--

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:53.248
whether I'm actually writing my
application in Node.js or Go.

00:10:53.248 --> 00:10:54.790
This effectively
just becomes, again,

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:56.138
part of the infrastructure.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.300
There are three main tenets
to Istio or service mesh.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:08.270
The first main tenet is
around uniform observability.

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:10.520
The second main tenet is
around operational agility.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.400
And we'll talk a little bit
about how that actually helps

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:14.660
and what you can do with that.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:23.750
And the last tenet is really
around policy-based security.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:27.550
So let's take a little bit of
a look around observability.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:29.270
So one of the things
with a service mesh

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:33.250
is, you tend to
deploy a sidecar proxy

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:35.077
co-located with every service.

00:11:35.077 --> 00:11:37.660
What that does is, it gives you
that nice consistent flow logs

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:40.360
and metrics across your
entire environment.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:43.780
From that, I get a very
good understanding.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:46.030
I get, effectively,
metrics out of the box.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:48.670
I haven't had to instrument
anything within my application

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:49.450
logic.

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:51.840
I can basically just
now deploy that sidecar.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.310
Wherever that sidecar is, I'm
getting those consistent flow

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:55.840
logs and metrics.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.240
The other thing that
we can do is we--

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:02.060
based on the actual
traffic flows,

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:05.050
we can get an understanding
of the topology

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:06.085
of the environment.

00:12:06.085 --> 00:12:08.210
This isn't something that
is a theoretical diagram.

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.420
This is now based on the
actual traffic that is flowing

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.040
through your environment.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.790
This is important for
a couple of reasons.

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:19.040
One is because, in
some cases, it's

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:22.610
sometimes even unclear as
to exactly what services

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.470
are communicating
with other services.

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:27.192
This gives you a representation
of what's really happening.

00:12:27.192 --> 00:12:29.150
And it gives you the
ability, potentially, even

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:32.330
to start locking down
your environment based

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:34.153
on those communication paths.

00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:35.570
It's actually kind
of hard to see.

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.237
It's a little washed
out on that screen.

00:12:37.237 --> 00:12:42.080
But there are lines
between all those circles.

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:44.705
The other key thing is we
can actually now construct

00:12:44.705 --> 00:12:48.260
SLOs in a consistent way
across all the services

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:49.510
within that mesh.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:51.200
And that's actually something
that Sandeep will actually

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:51.700
go through.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:57.850
So let's talk a little bit
about how you get started.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:01.580
So there are a bunch
of different ways

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:04.400
you can deploy Istio,
any way from just

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:09.080
going to the open source
repository using Helm Install.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.180
It's actually relatively
simple to get started.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:14.238
In doing so, in Kubernetes,
you would actually

00:13:14.238 --> 00:13:16.030
get something that sort
of looks like this.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:18.170
There's a bunch of
Istio components there,

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.340
Citadel's-- effectively
the CA components.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:25.100
You've got Galley, which does
all the configuration, Pilot,

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:29.010
which pushes all
the information down

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:32.763
to the sidecars,
telemetry, and policy.

00:13:32.763 --> 00:13:33.680
I don't see telemetry.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.780
I'm probably covering it.

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:39.020
That is, effectively, how
you can do policy checks

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:43.770
or integrate into back ends
in a very flexible manner.

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:46.880
Another key thing that's
important in the context of GKE

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:49.640
is Istio's sidecar injector.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:54.566
What that does is, this
allows you to turn on,

00:13:54.566 --> 00:13:58.250
on a per-namespace basis if
you choose to do so, where you

00:13:58.250 --> 00:13:59.955
want those sidecars injected.

00:13:59.955 --> 00:14:01.970
So this is a very
easy way to get

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:05.060
that proxy deployed for
every single workload

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:06.970
in that environment.

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:09.630
So what does that
actually look like?

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:12.020
So if I've actually
then deployed

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:14.590
a given service
within a namespace

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:18.740
that has the label inject,
the sidecar, when you actually

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:20.810
drill down into the
container itself,

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:24.800
you not only get the
application that's running.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:27.260
But you also get that sidecar
just automatically injected.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:29.300
So it becomes very,
very simple to deploy.

00:14:31.815 --> 00:14:33.940
By the way, all these
screenshots that I'm actually

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:36.330
showing-- the last couple
have actually been on GKE--

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:40.078
but the previous ones are
all on an alpha program

00:14:40.078 --> 00:14:41.620
that we actually
have available, too.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.787
So if anyone is interested
in trying this stuff out,

00:14:43.787 --> 00:14:45.100
please come see me afterwards.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:48.973
And we'd be more than happy
to have a conversation.

00:14:48.973 --> 00:14:50.890
And once you've done
that, you can effectively

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:52.125
then start monitoring.

00:14:52.125 --> 00:14:53.250
You're actually good to go.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:00.280
So this is effectively what
service managing gets you

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:01.450
from a--

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.210
going through that process,
deploying your service mesh--

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:07.420
it gives you these consistent
things, again, very cleanly,

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:08.442
out of the box.

00:15:08.442 --> 00:15:10.900
This diagram's a little bit
better because the actual lines

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.160
are showing up.

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:16.570
But I think this
is kind of like--

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:19.690
one of the key things that
we're really focusing on here

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.120
is really embedding
this capability

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.940
within the infrastructure itself
to make it easy for people

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:27.640
to adopt this and start
monitoring their services

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:29.330
at the service layer.

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:30.850
So at this point,
I'd actually like

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:34.865
to introduce Sandeep
to come up and--

00:15:34.865 --> 00:15:36.240
SANDEEP PARIKH:
Thank you, David.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:39.120
DAVID MUNRO: --talk about
setting up some best practices.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:40.300
SANDEEP PARIKH: Awesome.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:41.940
Oh, can we go back
to the slides?

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:43.380
Not quite right
for the demo yet.

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:44.310
Thank you.

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.740
So how do you take everything
David just talked about

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.050
with Istio and
service mesh and start

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:51.510
applying some of those
SRE's best practices

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:53.950
to your services?

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:57.150
So remember, what automation
does Istio give you out

00:15:57.150 --> 00:15:58.410
of the box?

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.470
It, by default, gives you
this uniform observability,

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.930
telemetry of logs,
traces, metrics

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:07.640
for all of your containers where
that sidecar proxy's injected.

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:09.890
It also gives you this
operational agility component--

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:12.560
so things like managing
traffic, rate limiting,

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.850
doing circuit breakers,
injecting faults--

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:17.300
you can do all that with Istio.

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:18.980
And it gives you a
single point of entry

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:22.723
for implementing policy
and security across, again,

00:16:22.723 --> 00:16:24.890
either your entire cluster
or your entire namespace.

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:26.420
And that could be five services.

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:27.693
It could be 500.

00:16:27.693 --> 00:16:29.360
So it gives you a lot
of this automation

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:32.570
to really apply policy at
scale across a large number

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:34.190
of infrastructure.

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:37.010
But let's focus on
observability here.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:40.040
How do you make practical
use of the observability

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:42.920
and telemetry that Istio
provides by default?

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:46.720
And how do you use
that in your own work?

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:49.730
So let's recap again
really quickly.

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:53.310
SLIs are the metrics that
you'll need to track.

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:56.250
SLOs are the targets
that you want to achieve.

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.990
For example, you may say,
for a given service--

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:01.050
if you remember that
complex diagram earlier--

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:03.300
you may say you want
the cart service

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:07.710
to have 99% availability
over the course of a week.

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:10.859
Or you may want the front
end to have 99% availability.

00:17:10.859 --> 00:17:14.670
But maybe the AdService could
be 90%, or 95%, availability

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:16.000
for a given week.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:17.875
So those are the kinds
of targets that you're

00:17:17.875 --> 00:17:18.970
going to want to set.

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:20.890
The SLAs, then,
are finally the--

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:22.910
after you do enough
of this work,

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:25.780
what are the sorts of business
and end-user guarantees

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:27.640
can you make about
those services

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.820
that you've instrumented?

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:32.740
So the first part
of this process

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:35.770
is figuring out which
SLIs do you care about?

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:38.240
There's a whole bunch that
Istio provides out of the box.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.460
Which ones are the ones
that are important to you?

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:43.700
And it's going to be different
on a service-by-service basis.

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:46.210
For some, latency may be
very critical for something

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:47.470
like the ad-serving.

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:49.480
Maybe for the front end,
latency is critical.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:51.767
But maybe the front end
also needs availability.

00:17:51.767 --> 00:17:54.100
Some others might just need
availability versus latency.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:55.570
It's a little bit
of a trade-off.

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:57.310
But the ultimate
idea is you want

00:17:57.310 --> 00:17:59.980
to decide and take a good look
at those services in depth

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:02.290
and figure out which
metrics are the ones that

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.607
are most important for
that particular service.

00:18:04.607 --> 00:18:06.190
Again, thinking about
how that's going

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:08.380
to impact the
end-user experience

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:11.740
or your other internal
customer experience--

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:14.650
your internal customers may
be other teams, other services

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:16.580
that they're running,
other dependencies.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:18.455
So that's where you want
to take into account

00:18:18.455 --> 00:18:20.470
all of that information
to decide and determine

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:23.710
which SLIs are important.

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.550
And then again, back to
SLOs, which ones are, again,

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:28.830
the ones that are most
important for those workloads?

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.413
Which ones are the
ones that are going

00:18:30.413 --> 00:18:32.930
to be very obviously
impacting anything downstream

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:34.530
or upstream from you?

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:36.390
So that's an important piece.

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:38.450
And then, finally, how
do you use all that?

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:39.825
How do you put
that all together?

00:18:39.825 --> 00:18:42.410
Well, the challenge with SLAs
is that it's a little bit

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:43.280
subjective.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.003
It's not a complete sort
of mathematical operation.

00:18:46.003 --> 00:18:47.420
There's a little
bit of work there

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:50.300
you can use to figure out,
well, if I know my SLIs,

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:52.462
if I know the targets
I'm trying to achieve,

00:18:52.462 --> 00:18:54.670
I can use that to figure
out what my error budget is.

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:56.900
I could say, well,
I can afford to have

00:18:56.900 --> 00:19:00.840
60 minutes of downtime in a
given month or a given year.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.020
So you know what
you're working with.

00:19:03.020 --> 00:19:04.700
But from that
point forward, what

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:06.380
that final guarantee
you have to make

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:08.060
is still a little
bit subjective.

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:09.780
You have some data
to back it up.

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:11.738
But there's still a little
bit of determination

00:19:11.738 --> 00:19:13.490
to make before you can
make that guarantee

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:19.160
or that promise to
internal/external customers.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.070
So what are the SLIs
that you get out

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.990
of the box with service
mesh and with Istio?

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:25.790
So you get six main things.

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:28.060
And these things are
dependent a little bit

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:32.440
upon what type of service you
have, whether it's HTTP or TCP.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:34.365
But regardless of
whether it's L4 or L7,

00:19:34.365 --> 00:19:36.490
you get these metrics,
again, right out of the box.

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:39.740
So that includes things
like request count,

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:43.540
the duration of the request,
the size, the response

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:47.015
size as well, or TCP
bytes sent and received.

00:19:47.015 --> 00:19:48.890
And by the way, there
are variations of these

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:50.960
for both inbound and outbound.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:53.420
So you get to know this
from the perspective of,

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:55.970
whether it's a source
part of the workload

00:19:55.970 --> 00:19:57.668
or a destination part
of that workload.

00:19:57.668 --> 00:19:59.960
And you have some ability to
control which ones, again,

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:01.060
are most important to you.

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:07.460
The important part of this is
that your applications don't

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:10.320
have to emit these at all.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:13.160
This is all done inside
that sidecar proxy.

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:16.420
So when you deploy your
service, and that Istio sidecar,

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:19.250
or that proxy, is injected
along with it, because it's

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:20.870
functioning at
the network layer,

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:23.420
it's automatically capturing
all this information

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:26.090
and then sending it to
those telemetry back ends.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:28.700
And those telemetry back
ends are then pushing it down

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:31.790
to the downstream monitoring
services like Prometheus

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.693
and Grafana, like
Stackdriver, like Datadog.

00:20:34.693 --> 00:20:36.110
And there's a whole
mix of others.

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:42.620
So now that we know a
little bit about the SLIs,

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:46.040
what does it mean to start
tracking health in depth?

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:48.800
That's specifically
around the SLOs.

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:50.750
So you have to make a
few decisions as you're

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:52.590
trying to create these targets.

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:54.750
The first is, what is the type?

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:57.038
What kind of SLI
am I worried about?

00:20:57.038 --> 00:20:58.580
And we give you two
options right out

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:01.010
of the box inside of the
Service Management alpha,

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:05.110
which is around latency
or availability.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:08.830
And then, on the SLO side, on
that specific compliance piece,

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:12.070
you have to decide, what are
the goals I'm trying to achieve?

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:15.553
90%, 95%, 99%?

00:21:15.553 --> 00:21:16.720
That's your compliance goal.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:18.730
And then you have to
assign a window of time

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:20.860
to that, or a period
of time, whether you

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:24.100
want it to be a calendar week,
or a rolling week-by-week

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:27.637
basis, or a day-by-day
basis, or even up to a year.

00:21:27.637 --> 00:21:29.220
And then, finally,
you have the option

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:30.420
of setting up windowed SLI.

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:32.670
So that actually adds
a little bit of-- it's

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:33.670
an optional piece there.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:35.850
But it adds a little
bit more specificity of,

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:40.530
I want to achieve a particular
metric compliance goal

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:42.212
in a smaller window of time.

00:21:42.212 --> 00:21:43.920
And then it'll aggregate
those windows up

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:46.323
to see if you're
reaching your compliance.

00:21:46.323 --> 00:21:48.240
The documentation does
a little bit better job

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:51.150
of explaining it, and with a few
examples, but the idea being,

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:53.400
you have different
ways of evaluating

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:56.190
how you want to track those SLIs
and how you want that to add up

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:59.840
to your final compliance goal.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:01.915
So we look at a few
examples of this.

00:22:01.915 --> 00:22:03.540
And these are somewhat
interchangeable.

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:05.580
So don't just go by the rows--

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:06.580
those only pieces there.

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:11.060
But for example, we
could set a latency SLO

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:15.770
where we want to hit 90% of
all requests, or all responses,

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:17.300
are within 100 milliseconds.

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:21.670
And we want that to be over
a rolling five-day period.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:26.590
And we also want it to hit
99% of those in an hour.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:30.880
So what that really means
is, for a given hour,

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:35.650
99% of the requests have to hit
that 100-millisecond milestone.

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:37.180
And then the
aggregate, that 90%,

00:22:37.180 --> 00:22:39.378
kind of rolls up from there.

00:22:39.378 --> 00:22:41.420
On the next line, we have
an availability example

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.600
where we want to hit, again, 99%
availability within a calendar

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:45.350
week.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:47.040
And we don't need
to window that one.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:49.393
So we have a little bit
of room to figure out,

00:22:49.393 --> 00:22:50.810
what's the right
combination here?

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:56.160
But really, again,
when you get back

00:22:56.160 --> 00:23:00.533
to that final piece
around SLAs, you

00:23:00.533 --> 00:23:01.700
can't make those guarantees.

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:03.680
You can't make those
promises until you

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:08.220
adopt SRE as a regular, ongoing
practice in your organization.

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.790
So the first thing really
is, not just deciding

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:14.460
which SLIs are important, but
exploring them fully, making

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:16.460
sure you have a really
good understanding of how

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:19.280
those metrics are functioning,
what those outcomes look like,

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:21.650
what that output looks
like and using that

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:24.020
and deciding which ones
are the most important.

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:25.610
You can't really--
you effectively

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:27.080
could monitor everything.

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:31.975
But that might be missing
the forest for the trees.

00:23:31.975 --> 00:23:34.350
You want to pick the ones that
are most important to you.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:36.100
So you want to make
sure you have a really

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:38.300
thorough understanding,
after a lot of testing,

00:23:38.300 --> 00:23:41.520
around those SLIs.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:43.790
And then you want to
set realistic targets.

00:23:43.790 --> 00:23:45.770
Go back to that
example earlier of,

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:48.650
not every service needs the
same level of availability

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:50.930
or maybe the same level
of latency guarantees.

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:52.347
You might have to
make trade-offs.

00:23:52.347 --> 00:23:55.390
You might not have a
5,000-person team to work with

00:23:55.390 --> 00:23:55.890
here.

00:23:55.890 --> 00:23:57.860
So you might have
to decide and say,

00:23:57.860 --> 00:24:00.903
which ones am I going to
guarantee are 99% available?

00:24:00.903 --> 00:24:02.820
Which ones am I going
to guarantee that I have

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:06.040
100-millisecond latency at 90%?

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:09.820
And with enough exercise
of those first two--

00:24:09.820 --> 00:24:12.340
understanding those metrics
in-depth and setting those

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:13.630
targets--

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:15.320
after you do enough
of that work,

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:18.190
then you can start to get to
the next part where you decide.

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:20.523
All right, here is my
error budget for the year.

00:24:20.523 --> 00:24:21.940
Here's how I can
make that, again,

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:23.785
that final guarantee
to my end user

00:24:23.785 --> 00:24:24.910
or to my internal customer.

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:32.090
So with service management
alpha, what we tried to do here

00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:34.930
is not just surface
all the data.

00:24:34.930 --> 00:24:37.010
I think that's the
first easy part, right?

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:39.920
Giving you all that data an
easy-to-consume dashboard.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:41.000
That's great.

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.730
We did that first
really important piece.

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:46.130
But the next part of it
is to give you the tooling

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:47.942
to go a level deeper.

00:24:47.942 --> 00:24:49.400
And I don't mean
sort of one level.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:51.070
I mean all the way through.

00:24:51.070 --> 00:24:54.470
And our first foray here is
really around observability.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:57.110
But as time goes
on, you could see

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:59.482
how that could evolve
to implementing

00:24:59.482 --> 00:25:01.190
more around even just
some of the agility

00:25:01.190 --> 00:25:03.382
or some of the policy and
security pieces as well.

00:25:03.382 --> 00:25:05.090
But we wanted to start
with observability

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:07.280
because that's a really
critical amount of data.

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:09.680
And frankly, what we
hear from a lot of people

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:13.280
is, their first real reason
for getting into Istio,

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:15.200
getting into service
mesh, is because they

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:16.730
want that observability.

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:19.580
They want that level of detail
they can't get right now.

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:21.110
Or they want that
level of detail

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:23.120
that individual
teams have, but they

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:25.340
don't have it in a
uniform way across

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:28.117
their entire environment.

00:25:28.117 --> 00:25:30.200
So from this dashboard,
the first thing you'll see

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:33.110
is some important pieces
across the top here--

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:35.172
services with alerts
firing, services

00:25:35.172 --> 00:25:36.380
that are out of error budget.

00:25:36.380 --> 00:25:37.970
And this is a
default deployment.

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:40.590
I just ran a couple of
things here as an example.

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:42.770
So it's not much
happening in this example,

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:45.080
but it's a starting point.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:47.420
But then beneath that,
beneath that first level

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.280
of information, we show
you all the services

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:52.310
across clusters,
pods, namespaces

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:53.540
that are running right there.

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:56.660
And you have the ability to
filter down and dig into them.

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:58.880
And this table's
actually very active

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.430
where different lines
can be read or have

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:03.290
different little check
marks of showing you

00:26:03.290 --> 00:26:06.560
which things are in danger
or running low on budget.

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:11.968
As you dig into an
individual service,

00:26:11.968 --> 00:26:13.510
we'll show you a
timeline at the top.

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:15.607
And that timeline
actually shows you--

00:26:15.607 --> 00:26:17.690
right now, it's blue because
there's no incidents.

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:19.400
But if there were
incidents or issues,

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:21.650
there are hard tick marks
there that you can dig into.

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:23.070
And you can narrow
that timeline and say,

00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:24.890
well, I want to focus
on that one incident.

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:26.612
What happened during that time?

00:26:26.612 --> 00:26:28.070
And then you have
the ability to go

00:26:28.070 --> 00:26:31.400
through the individual
metrics or diagnostics.

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:33.133
The diagnostics include
things like logs.

00:26:33.133 --> 00:26:34.550
You can look at
the infrastructure

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:36.300
specifically, like
which pods are actually

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:38.060
running the service.

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:40.580
And you could see some of the
inbound and outbound traffic

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:42.180
as well.

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.960
So you have the ability to dig
in here a little bit further.

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:46.490
And we surface a
lot of the data.

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:49.910
But we're trying to make it
easy, not only to see all of it

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:52.730
in action, but also take
action on it very quickly.

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:58.990
So I talked a little
bit about this.

00:26:58.990 --> 00:27:00.940
What's interesting is
that everything you need

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:03.340
to see and investigate is here.

00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:07.360
But in reality, once
you've spent enough time

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:10.937
understanding your SLIs,
setting realistic targets,

00:27:10.937 --> 00:27:12.520
you really shouldn't
be spending a lot

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:14.740
of time digging into those
services again and again.

00:27:14.740 --> 00:27:17.825
If anything, once you've
got really good SLOs,

00:27:17.825 --> 00:27:20.200
you probably don't have to
spend a lot of time going back

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:21.700
through the metrics
again and again.

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:24.340
Because the important thing
that you need to know,

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:27.310
that you're out of error budget,
or that you're potentially

00:27:27.310 --> 00:27:29.620
hitting outside of
your SLO boundaries,

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:32.342
that's going to be
surfaced up here as well.

00:27:32.342 --> 00:27:34.300
So you'll see some of
that stuff automatically.

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:35.830
So you shouldn't
spend too much time,

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:37.570
once you've got
everything up and running,

00:27:37.570 --> 00:27:39.445
having to reconfigure
things again and again.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:44.290
So how do you-- again,
we talked, again--

00:27:44.290 --> 00:27:47.190
we tried to take you from
what Istio provides you out

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:50.300
of the box, how you turn
that into SLO, SLIs,

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:51.845
and eventually SLAs?

00:27:51.845 --> 00:27:53.720
Well, how do you do
this, again, in practice?

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:55.762
How do you get more and
more of that specificity?

00:27:55.762 --> 00:27:59.780
So let's start with, again, that
reasonably complex workload.

00:27:59.780 --> 00:28:02.810
This is the online
commerce app that we built.

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:04.483
And how do we
start to understand

00:28:04.483 --> 00:28:05.400
what's going on there?

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:08.600
And let's pick a service
maybe that has some high tail

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:09.500
latency.

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:11.070
And first, we'll
examine all of them.

00:28:11.070 --> 00:28:13.550
Then we'll pick something
that we want to drill into.

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:17.930
We'll dive deep into it
and create an SLO around it

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:20.585
after we evaluate
some of those metrics.

00:28:20.585 --> 00:28:22.610
So if we could switch
to the demo machine--

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:30.660
all right, so here I've got the
service mesh/service management

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:32.100
Console up.

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:34.236
And can you guys see that OK?

00:28:34.236 --> 00:28:35.460
Yeah?

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:35.960
All right.

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:37.043
If you can't, let me know.

00:28:37.043 --> 00:28:39.020
Just stand up and wave.

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:40.608
Hopefully, I'll be
able to see you.

00:28:40.608 --> 00:28:42.150
So the first thing
we've got up here,

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:43.790
we've got a bunch of
services deployed.

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:46.370
And you can see things
across the top like where

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:49.810
no alerts are firing, where we
maybe have alerts configured,

00:28:49.810 --> 00:28:52.310
SLOs that are potentially
out of error budget, ones

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:54.890
that have no alerting
policies, or services

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:56.940
that have no SLO set.

00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:59.500
So let's look at some of
those with no SLO set.

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:01.590
So we'll filter by those.

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:02.640
And now we can see--

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:05.775
and let's pick something here
that has a high tail latency.

00:29:05.775 --> 00:29:08.910
I think CheckoutService
there has a high tail

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:12.208
latency at 99%, around
1,700 milliseconds.

00:29:12.208 --> 00:29:13.750
And that's probably
not what we want.

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:16.750
We want checkout to be
a much faster operation.

00:29:16.750 --> 00:29:18.750
So now we can dig into
that particular service.

00:29:24.558 --> 00:29:25.273
All right.

00:29:25.273 --> 00:29:26.440
Now we can see this through.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:28.065
And we can also pull
down that timeline

00:29:28.065 --> 00:29:29.750
if we want to take
a quick look at it.

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:33.800
And that time span can go
from an hour up to six weeks,

00:29:33.800 --> 00:29:36.380
depending on how far
back you need to look.

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:37.630
But we'll leave it at an hour.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:40.130
And before we get
to creating an SLO,

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:42.130
let's start taking a look
at some of the metrics

00:29:42.130 --> 00:29:44.747
and some of the diagnostics
that we surface up for you.

00:29:44.747 --> 00:29:47.080
Because right now, you'd be
in that investigative phase.

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:50.180
And you want to understand
what's happening.

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:52.668
So if we pull up the
metrics, we can see here--

00:29:52.668 --> 00:29:54.460
and I'm going to hide
this timeline for now

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:56.350
so we can see more of it.

00:29:56.350 --> 00:30:00.680
We break it down for you
by requests per second,

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:06.040
by the error rate, the latency,
and the request and response

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:07.382
sizes.

00:30:07.382 --> 00:30:09.340
And if we focused a little
bit here on latency,

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:12.100
we can see that, generally
speaking, latency

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:15.860
is looking around, let's say,
200 milliseconds roughly,

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:16.360
about 99%.

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:18.580
That's 255 milliseconds.

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:20.890
But we do have some spikes here.

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:25.750
So we are seeing things like
2 seconds in some cases.

00:30:25.750 --> 00:30:28.720
But we can change that
breakdown into, maybe,

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:30.430
by Request Operation.

00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:34.660
So we can see which
particular methods are

00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:37.770
driving some of that latency.

00:30:37.770 --> 00:30:40.320
So for example,
the CheckoutService

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:42.840
has a single method
called PlaceOrder.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:44.190
And so we could see that one.

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:47.120
And actually, I should mention
this CheckoutService is not

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:48.690
a HTTP service.

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:50.430
It's actually a gRPC service.

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:52.680
So this is servicing
gRPC calls just

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:54.180
like they were HTTP
methods, so it's

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:55.882
easy to browse through them.

00:30:55.882 --> 00:30:57.590
So we can go through
here and take a look

00:30:57.590 --> 00:31:00.050
around the latency piece.

00:31:00.050 --> 00:31:03.020
If we look at the
Request Size, we

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:04.580
can also change
that breakdown again

00:31:04.580 --> 00:31:08.270
by request operation
or request protocol.

00:31:08.270 --> 00:31:12.242
We could even base it on
what is the source workload.

00:31:12.242 --> 00:31:14.200
So in this case, all of
their incoming requests

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:16.420
for that service are driven
by the front end only.

00:31:21.620 --> 00:31:23.217
So that's a little
bit of that one.

00:31:23.217 --> 00:31:25.800
And then the last piece I wanted
to show you is the error rate

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:26.510
breakdown.

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:28.070
Again, because we
know the type here,

00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:30.500
we know it's gRPC or
HTTP, we can actually

00:31:30.500 --> 00:31:32.180
show you the HTTP
error code that's

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:33.510
coming back out the other way.

00:31:33.510 --> 00:31:35.760
So in this case, we've had
zero errors, which is good.

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:40.030
We want to see a low error rate,
obviously, for our services.

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.690
Now, on the Diagnostics
tab, what we'll show you

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:44.720
there are error logs.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:47.490
And we show you the
error log breakdown

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:49.680
by the types of
messages that you're

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:51.180
seeing in your error logs.

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:53.830
And these logs aren't really--
they're not really anything

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:55.622
in particular-- just
sort of default things

00:31:55.622 --> 00:31:58.435
that we put in there
for this example.

00:31:58.435 --> 00:32:01.060
The more interesting piece gets
into things like infrastructure

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:02.842
and connected services.

00:32:02.842 --> 00:32:04.300
When you expose
the Infrastructure,

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:06.385
you have the ability
then to dig into--

00:32:06.385 --> 00:32:07.760
all right, you
have a deployment.

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:08.552
You have a service.

00:32:08.552 --> 00:32:11.265
Well, inside that deployment
are multiple pods.

00:32:11.265 --> 00:32:12.640
Now we can see
each of those pods

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:15.950
and figure out which ones may
be driving some of those issues.

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:17.680
So we can see that
the high latencies are

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:23.258
coming from these two pods based
on these two rows in the table.

00:32:23.258 --> 00:32:26.780
Then we could also take a look
at the inbound and outbound

00:32:26.780 --> 00:32:28.950
traffic.

00:32:28.950 --> 00:32:30.570
As we can see, all
the inbound traffic

00:32:30.570 --> 00:32:33.870
is being driven by front end,
and all the outbound traffic

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:36.420
is going to other parts of--

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:39.082
so the CartService is
making outbound calls to--

00:32:39.082 --> 00:32:41.040
or, I'm sorry, Checkout's
making outbound calls

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:43.440
to the shopping cart,
CurrencyService,

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.690
sending emails after you
place an order, maybe

00:32:45.690 --> 00:32:47.790
the PaymentService.

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:50.230
And I don't want
to undersell this.

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:52.910
When we've worked with large
customer teams, where they've

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:55.310
had to take a large,
monolithic infrastructure

00:32:55.310 --> 00:32:58.495
and turn into microservices,
they've done that exercise.

00:32:58.495 --> 00:32:59.870
But what they
haven't realized is

00:32:59.870 --> 00:33:02.680
how many dependencies they've
picked up along the way.

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:05.180
So when we were talking-- we
were helping a customer migrate

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:09.260
from a microservices deployment
on-prem to Kubernetes,

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:12.620
we actually had them stand
up team-by-team and tell us

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:14.000
what their dependencies were.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:16.765
And as teams would stand up,
ones they didn't even expect

00:33:16.765 --> 00:33:18.140
would actually
highlight and say,

00:33:18.140 --> 00:33:20.330
actually, we're a
dependency on your service,

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:22.190
or you're a dependency
on our service.

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:23.732
And they didn't even
know about that.

00:33:23.732 --> 00:33:26.540
So this ability to observe
those inbound and outbound

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:29.192
connectivity is fantastic
for a lot of teams

00:33:29.192 --> 00:33:30.650
where they didn't
have this before,

00:33:30.650 --> 00:33:35.410
because their microservice
teams are very large.

00:33:35.410 --> 00:33:38.250
But now that we have some idea
of what those metrics look

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:41.910
like, let's actually go
back, and go back to Health.

00:33:41.910 --> 00:33:44.100
And let's create an SLO.

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:47.160
So when we do that, we're
going to first pick a type.

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:49.710
As I mentioned earlier, the
two that we surface by default

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:51.670
are availability and latency.

00:33:51.670 --> 00:33:53.520
So we'll pick Latency
because we know

00:33:53.520 --> 00:33:56.400
that's a problem for
the CheckoutService.

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:57.940
And we'll pick a low one here.

00:33:57.940 --> 00:34:01.740
Let's say we want a
100-millisecond latency.

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:05.540
We want that to be the
case 95% of the time.

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:10.199
And we want to set this to
be a Rolling 7-day Period.

00:34:10.199 --> 00:34:13.030
And so when you pick all that,
if you look on the bottom here,

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:14.110
we'll actually
automatically name

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:16.318
those for you, which does
add a level of convenience,

00:34:16.318 --> 00:34:18.250
if you want to glance
at these very quickly.

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:20.560
So 95% of all
requests need to be

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.540
less than
100-millisecond latency

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:24.603
in a rolling period
of seven days.

00:34:24.603 --> 00:34:27.469
Now, if we want, we can actually
add the windowed version here

00:34:27.469 --> 00:34:28.440
as well.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.199
So we can add a Window Target.

00:34:30.199 --> 00:34:33.020
So we want to say that,
within those time frames,

00:34:33.020 --> 00:34:36.889
we want 90% of all
windows that are

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:38.707
5 minutes long to meet that.

00:34:38.707 --> 00:34:40.040
And again, it updates that name.

00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:44.300
So now we can say 90% of all
5-minute windows in a rolling 7

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:48.195
days meet 95% latency.

00:34:48.195 --> 00:34:49.820
It's a little bit of
a mouthful, but it

00:34:49.820 --> 00:34:53.726
does encapsulate exactly what
we're trying to track here.

00:34:53.726 --> 00:34:55.960
I'm going to actually
take that one out.

00:34:55.960 --> 00:35:00.570
And we'll go ahead
and create this SLO.

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:03.660
And you could see right
away, we've got some status.

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:07.410
We're actually out of error
budget already with that setup.

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:10.600
So the service level
indicator is telling us

00:35:10.600 --> 00:35:15.940
that we're only hitting 5.9%
of that target instead of 95%.

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:18.627
We've actually burned, not
only partway through our error

00:35:18.627 --> 00:35:19.960
budget, but all the way through.

00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:23.500
We're actually negative by
about 5,000 minutes for the time

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:25.540
frame on our error budget.

00:35:25.540 --> 00:35:28.000
And we're only at
5.9% compliance.

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:31.158
So this immediately
is a bad situation.

00:35:31.158 --> 00:35:32.950
And from here, we
actually have the ability

00:35:32.950 --> 00:35:35.620
to create Stackdriver
alerting policies.

00:35:35.620 --> 00:35:38.940
So depending on
which SLOs, again,

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:41.810
are the most critical,
as part of your guarantee

00:35:41.810 --> 00:35:43.430
or your promise to
the end user, you

00:35:43.430 --> 00:35:45.770
may want to create
alerting policies.

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:48.620
Not every service being out
of error budget is equal.

00:35:48.620 --> 00:35:51.902
But I think having that, again,
that notification to your teams

00:35:51.902 --> 00:35:54.110
and saying, hey, we need to
address this immediately,

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:56.640
that's an important part
of this whole process.

00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:59.660
So if I were to hit that, it
would open a new tab here.

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:02.965
And that creates a
tab in Stackdriver.

00:36:02.965 --> 00:36:05.090
We're not going to wait
for that to load right now.

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:06.500
We'll come back to that one.

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:07.810
And then, why don't we do this?

00:36:07.810 --> 00:36:10.610
While we're here, let's create
another SLO around availability

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:13.450
because that's another important
metric we're worried about.

00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:16.282
So for Availability,
for the CheckoutService,

00:36:16.282 --> 00:36:16.990
this is critical.

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:18.040
This is how we get paid.

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:21.740
So we want 99% compliance here.

00:36:21.740 --> 00:36:25.290
And we want that to be
across a calendar month.

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:27.300
So for every month, we
want that to be the case.

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:29.540
So we need to hit
99% availability

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:32.353
in a calendar month.

00:36:32.353 --> 00:36:33.770
And we'll create
that one as well.

00:36:33.770 --> 00:36:35.530
Let me collapse this.

00:36:35.530 --> 00:36:38.030
And you can see, actually,
this one got created above.

00:36:38.030 --> 00:36:41.390
And we are currently
well within range.

00:36:41.390 --> 00:36:43.650
We're actually currently
at 100% availability.

00:36:43.650 --> 00:36:45.320
So we're above our target.

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:47.150
We've still got our
error budget remaining.

00:36:47.150 --> 00:36:50.030
99% ultimately leaves
you with 244 minutes

00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:51.373
where you could be down.

00:36:51.373 --> 00:36:53.540
So we're still doing great
on that one for the year.

00:36:53.540 --> 00:36:56.600
So that's good.

00:36:56.600 --> 00:37:01.910
Now, if we go back to the main
Service Management dashboard,

00:37:01.910 --> 00:37:05.140
we'll actually see this
highlighted here, specifically,

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:08.350
where the CheckoutService,
[? if ?] that's readable,

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:10.480
is out of error budget.

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:12.408
There's an SLO that's
out of error budget.

00:37:12.408 --> 00:37:14.200
And we can actually
filter by those as well

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:14.992
up here at the top.

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:19.490
So we can see now
individual services

00:37:19.490 --> 00:37:20.370
that are out of error budget.

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:21.980
So we could do the
same exercise here.

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:25.350
We can go explore the
ShoppingCartService

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:27.580
and, again, dig into this.

00:37:27.580 --> 00:37:29.830
Now, this one actually is
great because we've actually

00:37:29.830 --> 00:37:32.080
got some problems highlighted
in that timeline, right?

00:37:32.080 --> 00:37:33.340
You see some red marks there.

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:36.280
So we could actually narrow
the window on this timeline.

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:40.950
And let's pick this
incident here earlier today,

00:37:40.950 --> 00:37:43.975
and roll back-- this one.

00:37:43.975 --> 00:37:48.290
And now we can actually see
what some of the problem is.

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:52.620
We can dig into the individual
metrics during that time frame.

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:56.850
Can see there was a sharp drop
here, relatively speaking,

00:37:56.850 --> 00:38:00.147
in our requests per second?

00:38:00.147 --> 00:38:02.730
Maybe we want to take a look at
what the request operation was

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:03.430
that drove that.

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:08.157
So getting the cart suddenly
got dramatically slower.

00:38:08.157 --> 00:38:09.740
And again, we can
scroll through here.

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:12.050
We can see, at the same
time, we saw latency

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:13.745
go up somewhat dramatically.

00:38:13.745 --> 00:38:16.820
It scaled quite a
bit up from there,

00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:21.320
up to two seconds at the
99th percentile in one case.

00:38:24.337 --> 00:38:25.920
So this gives you
this ability, again,

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:28.170
to zoom in and dig around
and, again, develop

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:30.630
that investigative
understanding of those services.

00:38:30.630 --> 00:38:32.640
But really, you
probably wouldn't

00:38:32.640 --> 00:38:35.460
be spending time doing this
once you've set those SLOs.

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:38.340
But in order to set good ones,
you have to be realistic.

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:41.220
You have to know the
metrics really, really well.

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:42.750
So it's all a process.

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:46.440
But it becomes-- as you make
it a regular practice as part

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:50.850
of deployment, you get better
at deciding when to do,

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:53.110
as David mentioned earlier,
when to do code pushes,

00:38:53.110 --> 00:38:54.177
when you do deployments.

00:38:54.177 --> 00:38:56.260
Well, if you're out of
error budget for the month,

00:38:56.260 --> 00:38:58.720
you may want to hold off
and wait till the next month

00:38:58.720 --> 00:39:00.990
when you've got a little bit
more room to move around.

00:39:00.990 --> 00:39:02.000
And in that time
frame, maybe you

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:03.750
want to investigate
some of those metrics,

00:39:03.750 --> 00:39:07.208
again, to figure out what's
driving this problem.

00:39:07.208 --> 00:39:09.500
And one of the things we
mentioned, but we didn't show,

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:15.100
was tracing because that sidecar
works at the network level.

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:18.100
We automatically get traces
generated without instrumenting

00:39:18.100 --> 00:39:19.210
your code at all.

00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:21.355
So we didn't
instrument for logs.

00:39:21.355 --> 00:39:22.730
We didn't instrument
for metrics.

00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:24.820
And we don't even have to
instrument for tracing.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:27.070
But we could actually
trace inbound requests

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:28.630
and outbound requests
and figure out

00:39:28.630 --> 00:39:31.030
who's taking up the most
time in those requests

00:39:31.030 --> 00:39:32.817
without anything,
without any extra work.

00:39:32.817 --> 00:39:34.900
That's just something you
get right out of the box

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:36.025
with service mesh in Istio.

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:46.385
So that's a quick tour of
the Service Management UI.

00:39:46.385 --> 00:39:48.010
Could we go back to
the slides, please?

00:39:51.690 --> 00:39:52.190
All right.

00:39:52.190 --> 00:39:54.020
So how do you get
started with all this?

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:56.270
Well, the first
thing is, if you're

00:39:56.270 --> 00:39:59.060
really interested in digging
in, and testing out, and trying

00:39:59.060 --> 00:40:02.120
this UI, trying this
new functionality that

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:05.270
