00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:18.480
If you want to learn even
more and dig in further

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:21.360
around some of those
Istio core capabilities,

00:40:21.360 --> 00:40:22.940
I would definitely recommend--

00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:25.410
check out this "Welcome
to the Service Mesh Era"

00:40:25.410 --> 00:40:27.870
series that we wrote on
the Google Cloud Blog.

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:31.080
It goes through a bunch of
conceptual ideas around traffic

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:35.010
management, telemetry
with an SRE focus

00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:38.400
around how to roll
security out using Istio.

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:40.830
And there are actually
companion tutorials on GitHub

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:41.790
that go along with it.

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:44.368
So you could actually try
those examples out today.

00:40:44.368 --> 00:40:45.910
And then I would
definitely recommend

00:40:45.910 --> 00:40:49.300
you learn about
Istio's telemetry,

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:51.310
and what it, again, it
provides out of the box

00:40:51.310 --> 00:40:52.852
Because it does give
you quite a bit.

00:40:52.852 --> 00:40:54.880
Now, what I will tell
you is, it gives you

00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:57.530
quite a bit out of the box,
but you can always add more.

00:40:57.530 --> 00:41:00.580
You have the ability to add
in your own custom metrics

00:41:00.580 --> 00:41:03.030
that get captured by,
again, Prometheus, Grafana,

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:05.170
or Stackdriver, or
whatever the other services

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:06.295
that you might want to use.

00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:15.100
I would also ask for your
help in shaping this user

00:41:15.100 --> 00:41:16.060
